@@ -0,0 +1,59 @@
+# Android browser sign-in handoff
+
+This implements the GANY-28 Web/API side of the GANY-26 Android 0.1.2 contract. Deployment and device acceptance are separate gates. Do not treat passing local tests as a production login or App Links verification.
+
+## Protocol
+
+1. The app keeps a cryptographically random state and PKCE verifier locally. Open `/login?platform=android&android_state=<base64url>&android_challenge=<base64url-SHA256(verifier)>` in the system browser. State is canonical unpadded base64url encoding of 16–96 bytes; the challenge encodes exactly 32 bytes. The verifier follows RFC 7636 (43–128 unreserved characters). The Android contract uses 32 random bytes for state and verifier.
+2. The web page preserves state and challenge during email and Google sign-in. In this flow, `/auth/verify-code` and `/auth/google` receive `cookie_only: true`; success returns `{ "user": ... }` and HttpOnly auth/readable CSRF cookies, with no `token` field. Installed Desktop/CLI clients retain their existing login responses.
+3. With a valid human browser JWT cookie containing a session ID, send `POST /auth/android/prepare`, JSON `{ "state": "...", "code_challenge": "...", "app_id": "ai.multica.android" }`, credentials included and `X-CSRF-Token` copied from the existing CSRF cookie. Authorization header credentials, PAT/task credentials placed in cookies, forged identity headers and missing CSRF are rejected.
+4. The preparation response is `{ "redirect_url": "multica-android://auth/callback?code=...&state=..." }`. Only a random 256-bit single-use code enters the redirect. Its SHA-256 hash, user ID, source session ID/expiration, state, challenge and fixed app identity are stored in PostgreSQL. Its lifetime is the earlier of five minutes and the source cookie's expiration. Expired rows are removed during preparation.
+5. Android must verify the callback scheme/host/path and exact pending state before using the code. Post `application/x-www-form-urlencoded` fields `code`, `state`, `code_verifier` using WebView `postUrl` to **the same origin** `/auth/android/exchange`. A previous WebView cookie is not required. Native `postUrl` requests with a missing Content-Type are parsed as URL-encoded forms; other media types are rejected. An optional `app_id` must be `ai.multica.android`; omission means this fixed app. Query-string credentials cannot supply or replace form fields.
+6. The API atomically deletes the matching, unexpired row with `DELETE ... RETURNING`. Wrong proof does not consume a valid code. Replay, wrong stored app binding, expired source session and disabled/deleted users cannot establish a session. Success issues a new ordinary web session, sets `multica_auth` (HttpOnly) and both existing readable CSRF cookies, adds CDN cookies when configured, and responds **303 Location: /**. It returns no bearer token or JSON credential. Subsequent cookie-authenticated API writes still require CSRF.
+
+Native `WebView.postUrl` may omit `Origin`. A supplied Origin must equal `FRONTEND_ORIGIN`; browser requests marked `Sec-Fetch-Site: cross-site` are rejected to prevent planting an attacker's session. Opaque `Origin: null` is rejected. Device QA must capture the actual native POST headers; if the Android implementation sends an opaque Origin, use a same-origin WebView context before posting.
+
+`app_id` identifies a public client; it is not Android package attestation. PKCE binds redemption to the initiating verifier. Existing sessions are signed, stateless JWTs: logging out clears cookies rather than revoking server-side sessions, so an already prepared code remains usable until its short expiration. No new bearer-token persistence is introduced by this flow.
+
+## Routing and headers
+
+The Next runtime proxy and development rewrites forward `/auth/android/prepare` and `/auth/android/exchange` to Go. `/auth/android/callback` remains a Next page, alongside the existing Google `/auth/callback`; it forwards a validated HTTPS callback code/state to the fixed custom scheme as a browser fallback. The direct custom-scheme return is the default. After independently verifying domain association, the deployment owner can set the **API** runtime variable `ANDROID_AUTH_USE_APP_LINKS=true` (Helm: `backend.config.androidAuthUseAppLinks`) to return `https://<FRONTEND_ORIGIN host>/auth/android/callback` instead. This mode rejects missing or non-HTTPS frontend origins with 503. The HTTPS route or switch alone is not evidence that Android App Links work.
+
+Login/callback pages and handoff API responses use `Cache-Control: no-store` and `Referrer-Policy: no-referrer`. Sensitive client response-validation diagnostics redact payloads and custom error messages; Android callback logs omit raw error objects. The Go request logger records paths, not query strings or request bodies. Production ingress/CDN/APM owners must also disable body capture and query-string logging on auth/callback paths; never collect authorization codes, verifiers or auth cookies in evidence.
+
+## Deployment contract
+
+Apply migrations `735`–`737` before the API rollout. Both indexes use standalone concurrent-index migrations and register invalid-index cleanup hooks. Recheck numbering against the latest main before merge, regenerate with `make sqlc`, and run `go test ./cmd/migrate ./internal/migrations`.
+
+Configure the existing production `FRONTEND_ORIGIN=https://multica.ai` and appropriate `COOKIE_DOMAIN`; exchange must be accessed through `https://multica.ai/auth/android/exchange`, not the API origin. Ensure all Set-Cookie headers survive the proxy and auth POSTs are never cached. Retain the normal deployment's production JWT secret and Redis-backed auth rate limits.
+
+Set **the Next.js server runtime variable** `ANDROID_RELEASE_CERT_SHA256` to the public **release** signing certificate's SHA-256 fingerprint, formatted as 32 colon-separated hexadecimal bytes. Comma-separated fingerprints support certificate rotation. Docker self-host forwards this variable; Helm uses `frontend.config.androidReleaseCertSha256`. Never copy or request a private keystore. The release owner can export this public fingerprint from their signing system or public release certificate.
+
+`GET /.well-known/assetlinks.json` returns JSON with `delegate_permission/common.handle_all_urls`, namespace `android_app`, package `ai.multica.android` and the configured public fingerprints. It returns 503/no-store when fingerprints are absent or malformed; there is no fabricated production association or debug certificate default. A valid document is public and cached for five minutes. Serve it directly over HTTPS without redirects or authentication.
+
+Google's authorized redirect URI remains **https://multica.ai/auth/callback**, not the Android callback or exchange route. Official maintainers must confirm the existing deployed OAuth configuration supports that browser redirect; this flow does not require sharing a client secret or creating a separate Android OAuth client. Tests use a simulated provider and do not prove production Google configuration.
+
+## Acceptance handoff to GANY-26
+
+Required owner-provided inputs:
+
+- Public release certificate SHA-256 fingerprint for `ai.multica.android`; private signing material stays with its owner.
+- Official maintainers to deploy the Web/API changes, migrations, runtime fingerprint setting and domain association, and to verify ingress logging configuration.
+- Official maintainer confirmation that the existing Google OAuth configuration supports the exact browser redirect URI. Any configuration change can be made by its owner; no secret handoff is needed.
+- Authorized ADB device or working emulator with the intended signed APK.
+
+After authorized deployment, record the deployed revision, assetlinks status/content/fingerprint match, exchange status and cookie attributes without credential values, and browser-to-app-to-WebView behavior for email and Google. Verify deep-link state mismatch, replay, cold-start callback, cookie persistence after restart, authenticated CSRF writes and App Links association (`adb shell pm verify-app-links --re-verify ai.multica.android` and `adb shell pm get-app-links ai.multica.android`). Do not mark full Android login accepted until these device checks pass.
+
+Local verification entry points:
+
+```sh
+make sqlc
+go test ./internal/auth ./cmd/migrate ./internal/migrations
+# With the checkout's migrated, isolated DATABASE_URL:
+go test ./internal/handler -run 'TestAndroid|TestVerifyCode|TestGoogleLogin|TestGetMe' -count=1 -v
+go test ./cmd/server -run 'TestAndroidAuthRoutes' -count=1 -v
+pnpm --filter @multica/web test
+pnpm --filter @multica/views test -- auth/login-page.test.tsx
+```
+
+The independent test suite checks cookie-only email/Google login, cookie-backed exchange, anonymous/no-CSRF failures, state/verifier/app mismatch, source/code expiration, disabled users, replay and concurrent atomic consumption. Web tests own parameter propagation, redirect validation and proxy routing. Real Google and Android device behavior remain deployment acceptance checks.
